    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d176">

                <pb facs="0370" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v02/pg340"/>

                <head>176. Letter From President Eisenhower to Prime Minister Macmillan<anchor n="1" xml:id="d176-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1960-09-20"><hi rendition="#italic">September 20,
                        1960</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Harold</hi>: I am most
                    appreciative of your understanding letter of September 15. I have thought over
                    the important questions you raise and discussed them at some length with Chris
                    and Lord Home yesterday.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d176-f2"/></p>
                <p>As you say it is difficult to determine specific tactics until we see how
                        Khrushchev proposes to act and also
                    until we are able to assess the atmosphere at the General Assembly. I am
                    convinced that it is essential for our two delegations to concert closely and to
                    be prepared to move very quickly. Although it seems preferable to defer any
                    public <pb facs="0371" n="341" xml:id="pg341"/> announcement or firm decision on
                    timing for the moment, I now believe your attendance at the General Assembly
                    would be a real service to the West. I am impressed also by the desirability, in
                    the meantime, of having some outstanding Western leader answer Khrushchev and his satellites relatively early
                    in the proceedings. Mr. Menzies would be
                    superb in such a role and I hope you might be able to persuade him to attend,
                    preferably at an early date. I realize the problems you would face should
                        Khrushchev request a meeting with you
                    in New York. His behavior in New York may prove the best guide to your
                    answer.</p>
                <p>With warm regard,</p>
                <p>As ever,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Ike</hi><anchor n="3" xml:id="d176-f3"
                        /></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d176-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d176-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Washington National Records Center, RG 59, Conference Files: FRC 83–0068,
                    CF 1768. Secret; Presidential Handling. The text of the letter was transmitted
                    to London in telegram 1844, which is the source text. Telegram 1844 was repeated
                    to Herter at the USUN.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d176-f2" n="2" xml:id="d176-f2-content">
                <p>A copy of Macmillan’s letter to the
                    President is in Department of State, Presidential Correspondence: Lot 66 D 204.
                    Regarding Eisenhower’s conversation with
                    Herter and Home, see <ref
                        target="#d174">Document 174</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d176-f3" n="3" xml:id="d176-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram 1844 bears this typed signature.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
